**Configure structured output by passing a Pydantic model, Zod schema, or JSON schema to your agent's `response_format` parameter.**

When you configure a structured response format, the agent automatically captures and validates the output against your schema, returning the structured data in the agent's state (`structured_response` in Python or `structuredResponse` in JavaScript).

### Python (Pydantic)

```python
from pydantic import BaseModel, Field
from langchain.agents import create_agent

class ContactInfo(BaseModel):
    """Contact information extracted from text."""
    name: str = Field(description="The name of the person")
    email: str = Field(description="The email address")
    phone: str = Field(description="The phone number")

agent = create_agent(
    model="openai:gpt-4o",
    tools=[search_tool],
    response_format=ContactInfo
)

result = agent.invoke({"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Find info for Jane Doe"}]})
print(result["structured_response"])
```

### JavaScript (Zod)

```typescript
import { z } from "zod";
import { createAgent } from "langchain";

const ContactInfo = z.object({
  name: z.string().describe("The name of the person"),
  email: z.string().describe("The email address"),
  phone: z.string().describe("The phone number"),
});

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "openai:gpt-4o",
  tools: [searchTool],
  responseFormat: ContactInfo,
});

const result = await agent.invoke({
  messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Find info for Jane Doe" }],
});

console.log(result.structuredResponse);
```

LangChain automatically selects the best underlying strategy (such as provider-native structured outputs or tool-calling strategies) based on model capabilities.

**Relevant docs:**
- [Python Structured Output Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/structured-output)
- [JavaScript Structured Output Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/structured-output)

answer_1:
**To stream nested agents and subgraphs in LangGraph, use `stream_events()` with version `v3` or enable `subgraphs: true` when streaming.**

When working with multi-agent hierarchies or subgraphs, stream events bubble up through namespaces. You can inspect the event namespace to distinguish whether an update comes from the parent graph, a child subagent, or a grandchild subgraph.

```python
stream = parent_graph.stream_events({"my_key": "Bob"}, version="v3")

for event in stream:
    if event["method"] == "updates":
        # Namespace identifies the source (e.g., parent vs subagent)
        print(event["params"]["namespace"], event["params"]["data"])
```

## Streaming Subgraphs in Deep Agents

For Deep Agents with configured subagents, pass `subgraphs: true` in your stream config to receive real-time execution chunks from both the main agent and nested subagents:

```typescript
for await (const [namespace, chunk] of await agent.stream(
  {
    messages: [
      { role: "user", content: "Research quantum computing advances" },
    ],
  },
  {
    streamMode: "updates",
    subgraphs: true, // Enable subgraph streaming
  },
)) {
  if (namespace.length > 0) {
    // Subagent event - namespace identifies the source
    console.log(`[subagent: ${namespace.join("|")}]`);
  } else {
    // Main agent event
    console.log("[main agent]");
  }
  console.log(chunk);
}
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Subgraphs Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/use-subgraphs)
- [Deep Agents Streaming](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/streaming)

