**Use `wrapModelCall` middleware to inspect request context and dynamically swap the target model before each call.**

The `wrapModelCall` hook receives a `ModelRequest` object containing the current model, messages, tools, and runtime metadata. You can intercept the request and override the model parameter by returning a modified request to the `handler`.

```typescript
import { createMiddleware, createAgent } from "langchain";

const dynamicModelMiddleware = createMiddleware({
  name: "DynamicModelMiddleware",
  wrapModelCall: async (request, handler) => {
    // Determine the model dynamically based on state, metadata, or message length
    const messageCount = request.messages.length;
    const selectedModel = messageCount > 10 
      ? "anthropic:claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022" 
      : "openai:gpt-4o-mini";

    // Override the model on the request object
    const modifiedRequest = request.override({
      model: selectedModel,
    });

    return handler(modifiedRequest);
  },
});

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "openai:gpt-4o-mini", // Default model
  middleware: [dynamicModelMiddleware],
});
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Custom Middleware Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/middleware/custom)

answer_1:
**Use a `wrap_model_call` middleware with `request.override()` to dynamically filter which tools are exposed to the model on each turn.**

Register all tools with the agent executor so they can be invoked, but use middleware to show only a small core set plus a discovery tool initially. When the model invokes the discovery tool, update the active set of tools for subsequent turns:

```python
from langchain.tools import tool
from langchain.agents.middleware import wrap_model_call, ModelRequest, ModelResponse
from typing import Callable

ALL_TOOLS = [tool_a, tool_b, tool_c]
CORE_TOOLS = {"search_tools", "read_file", "write_file"}

@tool
def search_tools(query: str) -> str:
    """Search for available tools by keyword when you need a new capability."""
    matches = [f"- {t.name}: {t.description}" for t in ALL_TOOLS if query.lower() in t.name.lower()]
    return "\n".join(matches) if matches else "No matching tools found."

@wrap_model_call
def progressive_disclosure(
    request: ModelRequest,
    handler: Callable[[ModelRequest], ModelResponse],
) -> ModelResponse:
    active = set(CORE_TOOLS)
    for msg in request.state["messages"]:
        if hasattr(msg, "tool_calls"):
            for tc in msg.tool_calls:
                if tc["name"] == "search_tools":
                    q = tc["args"].get("query", "").lower()
                    for t in ALL_TOOLS:
                        if q in t.name.lower():
                            active.add(t.name)

    filtered = [t for t in request.tools if t.name in active]
    return handler(request.override(tools=filtered))
```

This prevents context bloat when working with large toolsets by progressively disclosing capabilities on demand.

**Relevant docs:**
- [Custom Middleware Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/custom)

answer_2:
**Use `wrapModelCall` middleware to inspect request metadata, runtime context, or state, and dynamically swap or override the model before each call.**

The `wrapModelCall` hook receives a `request` object containing the current invocation details. You can check runtime context (such as user tier, billing plan, or request parameters) and use `request.override({ model: ... })` to route the call to a different model dynamically.

```typescript
import { createMiddleware, createAgent } from "langchain";
import * as z from "zod";

const contextSchema = z.object({
  userTier: z.string(),
});

const dynamicModelMiddleware = createMiddleware({
  name: "DynamicModelMiddleware",
  contextSchema,
  wrapModelCall: async (request, handler) => {
    // Read user tier from runtime context
    const userTier = request.runtime.context.userTier;

    let selectedModel = "openai:gpt-4o-mini";
    if (userTier === "enterprise") {
      selectedModel = "anthropic:claude-3-5-sonnet";
    }

    // Override the model for this specific request
    const modifiedRequest = request.override({ model: selectedModel });

    return handler(modifiedRequest);
  },
});

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "openai:gpt-4o-mini",
  middleware: [dynamicModelMiddleware],
});
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Custom Middleware Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/middleware/custom)
- [Middleware Overview](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/middleware/overview)

**Pass your middleware array directly into `createAgent()` via the `middleware` configuration option.**

You can create custom middleware using `createMiddleware()` and define hooks like `wrapModelCall` to intercept and modify model requests, prompts, or messages at runtime:

```typescript
import { createMiddleware, createAgent } from "langchain";

const addContextMiddleware = createMiddleware({
  name: "AddContextMiddleware",
  wrapModelCall: async (request, handler) => {
    return handler({
      ...request,
      systemMessage: request.systemMessage.concat(`Additional context.`),
    });
  },
});

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "openai:gpt-5.5",
  systemPrompt: "You are a helpful assistant.",
  middleware: [addContextMiddleware],
});
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Custom middleware](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/middleware/custom)
- [Context engineering](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/context-engineering)

**Use a custom wrap-style middleware with `wrap_tool_call` to intercept tool execution and add retry logic.**

Wrap-style hooks give you full control over when and how the underlying tool handler is invoked, allowing you to catch exceptions and retry a specified number of times:

```python
from langchain.agents.middleware import wrap_tool_call, ToolRequest, ToolResponse
from typing import Callable


@wrap_tool_call
def retry_tool(
    request: ToolRequest,
    handler: Callable[[ToolRequest], ToolResponse],
) -> ToolResponse:
    max_retries = 3
    for attempt in range(max_retries):
        try:
            return handler(request)
        except Exception as e:
            if attempt == max_retries - 1:
                raise
            # Log or handle retry attempt
```

## Class-based Middleware Approach

You can also implement a reusable retry class by subclassing `AgentMiddleware` and defining `wrap_tool_call`:

```python
from langchain.agents.middleware import AgentMiddleware, ToolRequest, ToolResponse
from typing import Callable

class ToolRetryMiddleware(AgentMiddleware):
    def __init__(self, max_retries: int = 3):
        super().__init__()
        self.max_retries = max_retries

    def wrap_tool_call(
        self,
        request: ToolRequest,
        handler: Callable[[ToolRequest], ToolResponse],
    ) -> ToolResponse:
        for attempt in range(self.max_retries):
            try:
                return handler(request)
            except Exception as e:
                if attempt == self.max_retries - 1:
                    raise
                print(f"Retrying tool {request.tool_name} (attempt {attempt + 1}/{self.max_retries}) due to: {e}")
```

Pass the middleware instance directly when initializing your agent:

```python
from langchain.agents import create_agent

agent = create_agent(
    model="openai:gpt-4o",
    tools=[my_tool],
    middleware=[ToolRetryMiddleware(max_retries=3)],
)
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Custom Middleware Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/custom)

answer_1:
**Run code safely by using isolated sandbox environments or lightweight interpreters instead of executing arbitrary scripts directly on your host system.**

Sandboxes isolate agent execution so that arbitrary shell commands, package installations, and filesystem edits cannot compromise your local machine, credentials, or infrastructure.

```typescript
import { createDeepAgent } from "deepagents";
import { LangSmithSandbox } from "deepagents";

// Create a managed sandbox environment for secure execution
const backend = await LangSmithSandbox.create({ templateName: "my-template" });

const agent = createDeepAgent({
  model: "anthropic:claude-sonnet-5",
  backend,
  systemPrompt: "You are an expert developer working in a secure sandbox.",
});
```

## Choosing the Right Execution Pattern

Different security needs require different execution layers:

- **Sandboxes**: Use sandbox backends (such as LangSmith managed sandboxes, E2B, Daytona, or Modal) when agents need full OS filesystem access, package installs, git operations, or test execution.
- **Interpreters**: Use lightweight JavaScript interpreters (`eval` tool in a scoped QuickJS runtime) when the agent only needs pure in-memory loops, data transformations, or programmatic tool calling without shell or network access.

**Relevant docs:**
- [Sandboxes Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/sandboxes)
- [LangSmith Sandboxes](https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/sandboxes)

answer_2:
**Use `wrapModelCall` middleware with `request.systemMessage` to dynamically modify the system prompt before each model call.**

Read and modify the `systemMessage` field inside a custom middleware function to inject runtime context, user instructions, or environment details without altering the persisted conversation history:

```typescript
import { createMiddleware, createAgent } from "langchain";

const dynamicPromptMiddleware = createMiddleware({
  name: "DynamicPromptMiddleware",
  wrapModelCall: async (request, handler) => {
    // Fetch dynamic context at runtime (e.g., from request, store, or external API)
    const currentTime = new Date().toISOString();
    const dynamicContext = `Current UTC Time: ${currentTime}`;

    // Append context to the existing system message
    const updatedSystemMessage = request.systemMessage.concat(
      `\n\nAdditional Runtime Context:\n${dynamicContext}`
    );

    return handler({
      ...request,
      systemMessage: updatedSystemMessage,
    });
  },
});

const agent = createAgent({
  model: "openai:gpt-5.5",
  systemPrompt: "You are a helpful assistant.",
  middleware: [dynamicPromptMiddleware],
});
```

## Runtime Context and Schemas

Define a `contextSchema` when your middleware relies on structured runtime variables passed during agent execution:

```typescript
import * as z from "zod";
import { createMiddleware } from "langchain";

const contextSchema = z.object({
  userLocation: z.string(),
  userRole: z.string(),
});

type Context = z.infer<typeof contextSchema>;

const contextPromptMiddleware = createMiddleware<Context>({
  name: "ContextPromptMiddleware",
  contextSchema,
  wrapModelCall: (request, handler) => {
    const { userLocation, userRole } = request.runtime.context;

    const runtimePrompt = `User Location: ${userLocation}\nUser Role: ${userRole}`;
    const systemMessage = request.systemMessage.concat(`\n\n${runtimePrompt}`);

    return handler({
      ...request,
      systemMessage,
    });
  },
});
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Custom middleware](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/middleware/custom)
- [Custom middleware (Python)](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/custom)

answer_1:
**Prevent duplicate tool calls by maintaining an execution cache or idempotency layer within your tool definitions, or by managing state history.**

When an LLM accidentally invokes the exact same tool with identical arguments multiple times in a turn or across steps, you can use the `ToolRuntime` state to check recent tool calls or track execution status:

```python
from langchain.tools import tool, ToolRuntime

@tool
def get_user_data(user_id: str, runtime: ToolRuntime) -> str:
    """Fetch user data by ID."""
    # Check short-term state or executed tool calls to avoid duplicates
    messages = runtime.state.get("messages", [])

    # Simple deduplication check against recent messages
    for msg in reversed(messages):
        if getattr(msg, "tool_calls", None):
            for call in msg.tool_calls:
                if call["name"] == "get_user_data" and call["args"].get("user_id") == user_id:
                    return "Cached: Data already fetched in this conversation."

    return f"Fetched data for user {user_id}"
```

Alternatively, ensure your underlying API or database operation is idempotent so that accidental duplicate invocations do not produce side effects.

**Relevant docs:**
- [Tool Calling Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/models#tool-calling)

answer_2:
**Prevent duplicate tool calls by maintaining a state history of recent calls or using middleware to filter repeated executions.**

LLMs sometimes fall into loops where they reissue identical tool calls. You can prevent this behavior by inspecting past messages in your graph state or intercepting tool calls before execution.

```python
from langchain_core.messages import ToolMessage

def check_duplicate_tool_call(state):
    messages = state["messages"]
    last_message = messages[-1]

    # Check if the model is attempting any tool calls
    if not hasattr(last_message, "tool_calls") or not last_message.tool_calls:
        return {"messages": []}

    # Gather previous tool calls and their arguments
    previous_calls = set()
    for msg in messages:
        if hasattr(msg, "tool_calls") and msg.tool_calls:
            for tc in msg.tool_calls:
                previous_calls.add((tc["name"], frozenset(tc["args"].items())))

    # Filter out duplicate tool calls from the current request
    unique_calls = []
    for tc in last_message.tool_calls:
        call_signature = (tc["name"], frozenset(tc["args"].items()))
        if call_signature in previous_calls:
            # Return a canned response for the duplicate call
            return {
                "messages": [
                    ToolMessage(
                        content="Error: This tool has already been invoked with identical arguments.",
                        tool_call_id=tc["id"]
                    )
                ]
            }
```

## Handling Duplicates in Custom Tools

Alternatively, you can implement a cache or check inside the tool function itself using thread-local storage or checkpointer metadata to short-circuit repeated executions:

```python
from langchain.tools import tool

seen_requests = set()

@tool
def query_database(query: str) -> str:
    """Query the database for specific information."""
    if query in seen_requests:
        return "Duplicate call detected. Returning cached result."
    seen_requests.add(query)
    # Execute query logic...
    return "Query results..."
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Tools Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/tools)
- [Tool Calling](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/models)

**Configure progressive tool disclosure in Deep Agents by registering all tools upfront and using a `wrap_model_call` middleware to filter which tools the model sees on each turn.**

When an agent has dozens of tools, sending all definitions on every model call causes context bloat and increases latency. Progressive disclosure allows the model to start with a core set of tools plus a search mechanism, discovering additional tools dynamically as needed.

```python
from typing import Callable
from deepagents import create_deep_agent
from langchain.agents.middleware import ModelRequest, ModelResponse, wrap_model_call
from langchain.tools import tool

# All tools registered upfront for execution
ALL_TOOLS = [tool_a, tool_b, tool_c, ...]

# A small set always visible to the model
CORE_TOOLS = {"search_tools", "read_file", "write_file"}

@tool
def search_tools(query: str) -> str:
    """Search for available tools by keyword when you need a capability not currently visible."""
    matches = [
        f"- {t.name}: {t.description}"
        for t in ALL_TOOLS
        if query.lower() in t.name.lower() or query.lower() in t.description.lower()
    ]
    return "\n".join(matches) if matches else "No matching tools found."

@wrap_model_call
def progressive_disclosure(
    request: ModelRequest,
    handler: Callable[[ModelRequest], ModelResponse],
) -> ModelResponse:
    active = set(CORE_TOOLS)

    # Scan message history for prior search_tools calls
    for msg in request.state["messages"]:
        if hasattr(msg, "tool_calls"):
            for tc in msg.tool_calls:
                if tc["name"] == "search_tools":
                    q = tc["args"].get("query", "").lower()
                    for t in ALL_TOOLS:
                        if q in t.name.lower() or q in t.description.lower():
                            active.add(t.name)

    filtered = [t for t in request.tools if t.name in active]
    return handler(request.override(tools=filtered))

agent = create_deep_agent(
    model="anthropic:claude-sonnet-5",
    tools=[search_tools, *ALL_TOOLS],
    middleware=[progressive_disclosure],
)
```

## How It Works

The model starts with a minimal set of `core_tools` plus `search_tools`. When it encounters a task requiring a specialized capability, it calls `search_tools` with a keyword query. The middleware inspects the message history, identifies matching tools, and exposes them in subsequent turns via `request.override(tools=filtered)`.

**Relevant docs:**
- [Progressive Tool Disclosure with Deep Agents Support Article](https://support.langchain.com/articles/8488719552-progressive-tool-disclosure-with-deep-agents)

**Deep Agents subagents provide context isolation by running in an isolated execution mode (`mode: "isolated"`) where the subagent sees only the delegated task description and has no access to the parent's conversation history.**

When a main agent delegates a task via the `task()` tool, the subagent processes intermediate tool calls (such as web searches, file reads, or database queries) internally. The main agent receives only the final summarized output, preventing context bloat and keeping token consumption low.

```typescript
// Define a subagent with isolated context and specific tools
const researchSubagent: SubAgent = {
  name: "research-agent",
  description: "Perform web research and return a summary.",
  systemPrompt: "You are a focused researcher. Perform searches and return findings.",
  mode: "isolated", // Default: completely hides parent conversation history
  tools: [webSearch],
};

const agent = await createDeepAgent({
  model: "anthropic:claude-sonnet-5",
  subagents: [researchSubagent],
});
```

## Context Modes and Isolation Control

While isolation is the default (`mode: "isolated"`), subagents can be configured for different boundaries depending on the workflow requirements:

- **Isolated (`mode: "isolated"`):** The subagent starts with a blank context containing only its system prompt and the specific task description. It does not inherit conversation history or skills unless explicitly specified.
- **Forked (`mode: "fork"`):** If you need the subagent to continue an active workflow where the parent left off (such as drafting an incident postmortem), setting `mode: "fork"` allows the subagent to inherit the parent's full message history and system prompt.

**Relevant docs:**
- [Subagents Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/subagents)

**Deep Agents use pluggable backends to manage virtual filesystems for tools like `ls`, `read_file`, `write_file`, `edit_file`, `glob`, and `grep`.**

Choose your backend based on where and how long your files need to persist:

- **`StateBackend` (Default):** Thread-scoped scratch space stored in LangGraph state. Files persist across turns within a single thread via your checkpointer but are not shared across threads.
- **`StoreBackend`:** Long-term storage persisted across threads using a LangGraph store. Ideal for memories or project instructions spanning multiple conversations.
- **`FilesystemBackend`:** Directly accesses the local machine's disk under a configured root directory. Best for local development CLIs and CI/CD pipelines.
- **`CompositeBackend`:** Combines multiple backends by routing specific paths (e.g., routing `/memories/` to a `StoreBackend` while keeping everything else in `StateBackend`).

---

### Python Configuration Example

```python
from deepagents import create_deep_agent
from deepagents.backends import CompositeBackend, StateBackend, StoreBackend
from langgraph.store.memory import InMemoryStore

# Route default workspace to thread state, and /memories/ to cross-thread store storage
store = InMemoryStore()
agent = create_deep_agent(
    model="anthropic:claude-sonnet-5",
    backend=CompositeBackend(
        default=StateBackend(),
        routes={
            "/memories/": StoreBackend(namespace=lambda _rt: ("memories",)),
        },
    ),
    store=store,
)
```

### JavaScript / TypeScript Configuration Example

```typescript
import { createDeepAgent, CompositeBackend, StateBackend, StoreBackend } from "deepagents";
import { InMemoryStore } from "@langchain/langgraph";

const store = new InMemoryStore();

const agent = createDeepAgent({
  model: "anthropic:claude-sonnet-5",
  backend: new CompositeBackend(new StateBackend(), {
    "/memories/": new StoreBackend({
      namespace: () => ["memories"],
    }),
  }),
  store,
});
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Python Backends Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/backends)
- [JavaScript Backends Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/backends)

**Use `ToolStrategy` in your agent's `response_format` (or `responseFormat`) configuration to enforce a structured response schema via tool calling.**

Pass your Pydantic model, Zod schema, or JSON schema wrapped in `ToolStrategy` when creating your agent:

```python
from langchain.agents import create_agent
from langchain.agents.structured_output import ToolStrategy
from pydantic import BaseModel, Field

class ProductReview(BaseModel):
    """Analysis of a product review."""
    rating: int | None = Field(description="The rating of the product", ge=1, le=5)
    sentiment: str = Field(description="The sentiment of the review")
    key_points: list[str] = Field(description="Key points of the review")

agent = create_agent(
    model="gpt-5.5",
    tools=[],
    response_format=ToolStrategy(ProductReview)
)

result = agent.invoke({
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Great product: 5 out of 5 stars."}]
})

print(result["structured_response"])
```

```typescript
import * as z from "zod";
import { createAgent, toolStrategy } from "langchain";

const ProductReview = z.object({
    rating: z.number().min(1).max(5).optional(),
    sentiment: z.enum(["positive", "negative"]),
    keyPoints: z.array(z.string()).describe("Key points of the review"),
});

const agent = createAgent({
    model: "gpt-5.5",
    tools: [],
    responseFormat: toolStrategy(ProductReview)
});

const result = await agent.invoke({
    messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Great product: 5 out of 5 stars." }]
});

console.log(result.structuredResponse);
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Python Structured Output Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/structured-output)
- [JavaScript Structured Output Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/structured-output)

answer_1:
**Add capabilities on demand using progressive tool disclosure with a `wrap_model_call` middleware.**

Pass all available tools upfront so the agent can execute them, but filter the tool definitions visible to the model on each turn. Give the model a core tool set plus a search tool, allowing it to discover and load additional capabilities dynamically as needed.

```python
from langchain.tools import tool
from langchain.agents.middleware import wrap_model_call, ModelRequest, ModelResponse
from deepagents import create_deep_agent
from typing import Callable

# Register all tools upfront
ALL_TOOLS = [tool_a, tool_b, tool_c]

# Core tools always visible to the model
CORE_TOOLS = {"search_tools", "read_file", "write_file"}

@tool
def search_tools(query: str) -> str:
    """Search for available tools by keyword when you need a capability."""
    matches = [
        f"- {t.name}: {t.description}"
        for t in ALL_TOOLS
        if query.lower() in t.name.lower() or query.lower() in t.description.lower()
    ]
    return "\n".join(matches) if matches else "No matching tools found."

@wrap_model_call
def progressive_disclosure(
    request: ModelRequest,
    handler: Callable[[ModelRequest], ModelResponse],
) -> ModelResponse:
    active = set(CORE_TOOLS)

    # Scan message history for prior search_tools calls
    for msg in request.state["messages"]:
        if hasattr(msg, "tool_calls"):
            for tc in msg.tool_calls:
                if tc["name"] == "search_tools":
                    q = tc["args"].get("query", "").lower()
                    for t in ALL_TOOLS:
                        if q in t.name.lower() or q in t.description.lower():
                            active.add(t.name)

    filtered = [t for t in request.tools if t.name in active]
    return handler(request.override(tools=filtered))

agent = create_deep_agent(
    model="anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6",
    tools=[search_tools, *ALL_TOOLS],
    middleware=[progressive_disclosure],
)
```

## How It Works

1. The model starts with a minimal set of core tools and `search_tools` to avoid context bloat.
2. When the agent encounters a task requiring an unlisted capability, it invokes `search_tools`.
3. The middleware inspects the conversation history for `search_tools` calls and adds matching tools to subsequent model turns via `request.override(tools=filtered)`.

**Relevant docs:**
- [Custom middleware](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/custom)

answer_2:
**Delegate subtasks to specialized Deep Agents subagents by defining them in the `subagents` configuration array and calling them dynamically within code interpreter middleware.**

Configure custom subagents by passing a `name`, `description`, and `systemPrompt` to `createDeepAgent()`, then use the `task()` helper in interpreter code to fan out and dispatch tasks:

```ts
import { createDeepAgent } from "deepagents";
import { createCodeInterpreterMiddleware } from "@langchain/quickjs";

const agent = createDeepAgent({
  model: "openai:gpt-5.5",
  subagents: [{
    name: "reviewer",
    description: "Reviews code for security issues, citing lines and severity",
    systemPrompt: "You are a security-focused code reviewer. Report issues with line numbers and severity.",
  }],
  middleware: [createCodeInterpreterMiddleware()],
});
```

## Fan-Out and Synthesize

Once configured, the main agent can write code using the `task()` function to dispatch subtasks across subagents in parallel and combine their results:

```ts
// Dispatch tasks to the specialized reviewer subagent in parallel
const reviews = await Promise.all(
  files.map((file) =>
    task({
      description: `Review ${file} for security issues. Cite line numbers.`,
      subagentType: "reviewer",
      responseSchema: issuesSchema,
    }),
  ),
);

// Aggregate and process the returned results
const issues = reviews.flatMap((r) => r.issues);
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Dynamic Subagents Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/dynamic-subagents)
- [Deep Agents Subagents Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/subagents)

answer_1:
**Give an agent a workspace by configuring a sandbox backend, which provides an isolated filesystem and shell execution tools (`execute`, `read_file`, `write_file`, etc.).**

Pass a sandbox backend instance to the `backend` parameter when creating your deep agent:

```typescript
import { createDeepAgent, LangSmithSandbox } from "deepagents";
import { ChatAnthropic } from "@langchain/anthropic";
import { SandboxClient } from "langsmith/sandbox";

const client = new SandboxClient();
const lsSandbox = await client.createSandbox();

try {
  const agent = createDeepAgent({
    model: new ChatAnthropic({ model: "claude-opus-4-8" }),
    systemPrompt: "You are a coding assistant with workspace access.",
    backend: new LangSmithSandbox({ sandbox: lsSandbox }),
  });

  const result = await agent.invoke({
    messages: [
      {
        role: "user",
        content: "Create a workspace script and run it",
      },
    ],
  });
} finally {
  await client.deleteSandbox(lsSandbox.name);
}
```

**Relevant docs:**
- [Deep Agents Sandboxes](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/sandboxes)
- [Deep Agents Customization](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/customization#sandboxes)

answer_2:
**Use `FilesystemBackend` with a Deep Agent by passing an absolute `rootDir` and enabling `virtualMode: true` to sandbox file operations.**

Because Deep Agents write internal offloaded tool outputs and history files, we recommend wrapping `FilesystemBackend` in a `CompositeBackend` so your project files stay separate from ephemeral agent artifacts.

```typescript
import { createDeepAgent, CompositeBackend, FilesystemBackend, StateBackend } from "deepagents";

const agent = createDeepAgent({
  model: "google-genai:gemini-3.6-flash",
  backend: new CompositeBackend(
    new StateBackend(),
    {
      "/workspace/": new FilesystemBackend({ 
        rootDir: "/absolute/path/to/project", 
        virtualMode: true 
      }),
    },
  ),
});
```

**How it works:**

* **`rootDir`**: Specifies the base directory on your local machine that the agent can interact with. Must be an absolute path.
* **`virtualMode: true`**: Sandboxes and normalizes paths within `rootDir`, preventing traversal outside the root directory (blocking `..`, `~`, and absolute paths).
* **Composite Routing**: Maps specific virtual paths like `/workspace/` to your local disk via `FilesystemBackend`, while keeping internal agent data (such as large tool outputs) in ephemeral `StateBackend` storage.

**Relevant docs:**
- [FilesystemBackend (local disk)](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/backends#filesystembackend-local-disk)

**Pass an instance of `FilesystemBackend` to the `backend` parameter when initializing your Deep Agent.**

Import `FilesystemBackend` from the `deepagents` package, configure its `rootDir` and optional settings like `virtualMode`, and pass it to `createDeepAgent()`:

```ts
import { createDeepAgent, FilesystemBackend } from "deepagents";

const agent = await createDeepAgent({
  model: "anthropic:claude-sonnet-5",
  backend: new FilesystemBackend({ 
    rootDir: ".", 
    virtualMode: true 
  }),
});
```

## Wrapping in CompositeBackend

Wrap `FilesystemBackend` in a `CompositeBackend` if you want to keep internal agent data (such as conversation history and offloaded tool results) separate from your project files on disk.

**Relevant docs:**
- [Deep Agents Customization Guide](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/customization)
- [Deep Agents Backends](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/deepagents/backends)
